C = [
("h1", "15. What the Passages Do Not Say"),
("p", "Claim: much of what is said about the Cross is not in Paul’s sentences. The volume has argued that each passage is to be read as it stands, and this chapter sets out, as a report of the text, what the passages do not say. It does not build a doctrine from silence. It says where the sentences stop so that a reader does not mistake the end of a sentence for the end of a subject."),
("sub", "15.1 No single theory"),
("p", "None of the passages says that its figure is the one by which all the others are to be understood. Romans 3:25 does not say that propitiation is the key. Galatians 3:13 does not say that the curse is the key. Colossians 2:15 does not say that the triumph over the powers is the key. Each is a statement, and none is a thesis about the others. The volume’s own thesis, that Paul names several things the Cross did, is a report of the whole and a refusal to rank the parts."),
("sub", "15.2 No account of mechanism"),
("p", "The passages say that Christ died, was delivered, was made a curse, was made sin. They do not say how a death on one man’s behalf is received by another, what happened between the Father and the Son, or what the exchange consisted of. The volume does not read into the word “for” in “for us” more than the English gives. It does not claim that the question of mechanism is improper. It claims that it is not answered in these sentences."),
("sub", "15.3 No statement about the extent beyond the clauses"),
("p", "Four phrases are used of the persons: “for us” (Romans 5:8; Galatians 3:13), “for all” (2 Corinthians 5:14, 15; 1 Timothy 2:6), “for the ungodly” (Romans 5:6), and “for whom Christ died” (Romans 14:15; 1 Corinthians 8:11). Each is reported as written. The volume does not say that “all” means every person without exception, and it does not say that it means all kinds of persons. It says that Paul writes “for all” in two places and “for us” in several, and that in 2 Corinthians 5 he goes on at once to speak of the living and of the ministry of reconciliation. A doctrine of limited or of universal extent is not stated in any of them."),
("sub", "15.4 No statement about earlier believers"),
("p", "The passages are addressed to the readers of Paul’s letters. Romans 3:25 speaks of “sins that are past” and of the forbearance of God, and the volume has reported the clause as written. No passage says that Abraham, Moses or David were saved by believing that Christ died for their sins. No passage says that they were not. The volume does not decide the question. It holds that salvation is always by faith, that the content of faith is what God had revealed to the person concerned, and that the gospel Paul delivered, with the death of Christ for our sins at its front, is what the Body believes. It adds no shared saving ground between the Body and any earlier company."),
("sub", "15.5 No statement about the Body’s covenants"),
("p", "The passages on the blood and the Cross are addressed to the Body, and they say that the Body has redemption, forgiveness, nearness and peace. They do not say that the Body has entered a covenant of Israel’s. The blood of a covenant is a subject of the Supper and of the new covenant, which belong to other books. The volume reads the Body’s blood passages as the Body’s own. It reports the passages in Galatians 3 on the blessing of Abraham as stated, and keeps to the sentence that the Body receives blessing through Christ alone, not through participation in the Abrahamic covenant."),
("sub", "15.6 No statement about the powers"),
("p", "The passages that name principalities and powers say that they were spoiled and triumphed over (Colossians 2:15). They do not name the powers, and they do not say what the Body is to do about them. The volume does not infer from the verse a command to bind, rebuke or cast out any power. It reports that the verse contains none."),
("tbl", "What each passage stops short of.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 3:25", "“for the remission of sins that are past, through the forbearance of God”", "No account of who the past sins were, or how they were dealt with"),
    ("Gal 3:13", "“being made a curse for us”", "No definition of “us” beyond the passage"),
    ("2Cor 5:14", "“if one died for all, then were all dead”", "No statement that “all” are saved"),
    ("2Cor 5:21", "“hath made him to be sin for us”", "No account of how"),
    ("Col 2:15", "“having spoiled principalities and powers”", "No identification of the powers"),
    ("Eph 2:16", "“having slain the enmity thereby”", "No statement about covenant"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "15.7 Why the chapter matters"),
("p", "A reader who comes to the Cross wanting a single explanation will find the silences frustrating. The volume does not remove them. It asks the reader to see that the passages are not silent where silence would be a gap in the writing. They are complete in what they set out to say. The things they leave unsaid are things they were not written to say. What Paul says about the Cross he says in sentences with a subject, an object, a problem and a result, and the volume has tried to keep each sentence in its place."),
]
